    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d589">

                <pb facs="0651" n="547" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg547"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01 Manchuria/29: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-03-10T11:00:00+08:00">March 10, 1932—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 10—8:40 a.m.<anchor n="76" xml:id="d589-f1"
                        />]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>320. Following from American Consul General at Harbin: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“March 9, 11 a.m.</p>
                        <p>1. Yesterday Shao Lin, appointed by
                                Hsi Chia chief of the Harbin
                            branch of the Foreign Office of Kirin Province, called on me and stated
                            that he had been instructed by Hsi
                                Chia to hand me a written invitation which in translation
                            reads as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                                <p>‘The Executive of the Manchurian Government will be inaugurated
                                    at Changchun at 2 p.m., March 9th, the first year of Tatung. You
                                    are respectfully requested to attend the ceremony at the time
                                    appointed. The Northeastern Government Administration
                                    Committee.’</p></quote></p>
                        <p>I informed him that I could not accept this invitation because Changchun
                            is outside of my consular district and that without instructions of the
                            Department I could not attend celebrations of this sort.</p>
                        <p>2. Shao Lin wrote the official
                            letter dated March 7th containing a similar invitation and requesting a
                            reply which I have not sent.</p>
                        <p>3. The attitude of my consular colleagues except the Japanese is the <pb
                                facs="0652" n="548" xml:id="pg548"/> same as mine. Vice Consul
                                Takigawa proceeded yesterday
                            to Changchun to attend inauguration ceremony.</p>
                        <p>4. Decorative arches have been erected at various places and the
                            municipal government have ordered the celebration of “three hsin”<anchor
                                n="76a" xml:id="d589-f2"/> commencing today, but hardly anyone
                            except a few “White” Russians and self-seeking Chinese are taking
                            celebrations seriously. Pu Yi is
                            butt of many jokes among Russians and Chinese who realize his
                            inauguration is a farce.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d589-f1" n="76" xml:id="d589-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram in two sections.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d589-f2" n="76a" xml:id="d589-f2-content">
                <p>“Three <hi rendition="#italic">hsin</hi>” signifies good luck.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
